  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1032 PM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nAn upper low will descend over Southern California through the day \nMonday. As it gradually shifts south and eastward Monday night into \nTuesday, the already strong Pacific moisture fetch from \nassociated SW flow aloft will absorb the additional remnant \nmoisture from Hurricane Polo which will all be advected into our \nvicinity. Although models are still coming into a consensus, the \ngeneral thinking remains the same from previous forecasts. Rain \nshould begin early Tuesday morning near the TX/NM border and \ngradually track eastward through the day. Again model timing \nvaries, however heaviest rain is expected to overspread the area \nat some point from Tuesday evening to Wednesday morning before a \ndry slot builds in as the low occludes. Also of note is the \nassociated surface low positioned to the west on Tuesday combined \nwith a fairly strong low-level jet will bring breezy southerly \nwinds during the afternoon, potentially gusting up to 40 mph. A \ntrough moving over central Canada will push the upper low back due\nsouthward Wednesday into Thursday and depending on the exact \ntrack, further precipitation is possible along its backside \nThursday into Friday. All this to say, there remains much \nuncertainty this week, but in a nutshell heavy rainfall and \nlocalized flooding are possible (with slightly higher chances \ngenerally on the Caprock). We will continue to monitor and update \nthe forecast as needed.",
